  "title": "Bruxelles: From Comic Books to the EU",
  "date": "2026-01-18T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Bruxelles: From Comic Books to the EU\n\nThe Classic \"DB Experience\" 🚆\n\nTypical Deutsche Bahn—my train to Brussels was delayed. Traveling from Cologne, I first arrived at Brussels North. I learned that my transfer ticket to Brussels Central was valid for 24 hours, meaning I could hop on any train heading that way.\n\nThe challenge? The platform screens were strictly in French and Dutch, and neither mentioned \"Brussels Central\" as a stop (it's usually a passing station). Thanks to a quick assist from Gemini, I managed to navigate the linguistic puzzle and find the right platform! 💡\n\nThe platform\n\nHills, Statues, and Grand Squares 🏛️\n\nI made a slight tactical error today. Cologne was so walkable that I assumed Brussels would be the same. I was wrong—this city has a lot of hills to climb! 🏃‍♂️💨\n\nI started at the Grand Place, which is just a short walk from the Central Station. The square is absolutely breathtaking! Nearby, I tracked down the famous Manneken Pis (pissing boy) and Jeanneke Pis (pissing girl) sculptures.\n\nGrand Place\n\nA Change of Plans: The Comic Museum 🎨\n\nThe Comic Art Museum wasn’t originally in my schedule. I only had a 3-4 hour window in the city, and it seemed a bit out of the way. However, I realized I’d regret skipping it, so I made a last-minute detour. It was definitely worth the stop! 📚\n\nComic museum\n\nScientific History at the EU Council 🇪🇺\n\nNext, I headed over to the EU Council. Since it was Sunday, the area was peaceful and empty. But the real goal wasn't just the politics—it was science history.\n\nEU council\n\nI was searching for the location of \"The Most Intelligent Picture Ever Taken\" (the 1927 Solvay Conference). It was taken right near the EU Council buildings. Standing where Einstein and Curie once stood felt legendary. 🧪🧠\n\nthe Most Intelligent Picture Ever Taken\n\nQuick Bites & Station Vibes 🍟\n\nEveryone in Brussels seems to be eating Belgian fries (Fritkot). While it's the local staple, I skipped them this time—mostly because I was still working through a giant amout sausages I brought from Berlin! 🌭\n\nNext Stop: Paris with OuiGo 🇫🇷\n\nMy journey continued from Brussels South (Midi/Zuid). This station is huge, handling everything from local lines to the Eurostar.\n\nBrussel Midi\n\nI took the OuiGo to Paris, which is a budget traveler's dream if you're prepared. They use \"second-hand\" trains and charge extra for everything—luggage, power outlets, and Wi-Fi. However, the seats are surprisingly spacious! The desk was actually large enough to hold my laptop comfortably, which is rare for European trains. 💻\n\nTip for travelers: I arrived an hour early expecting a security pre-check, but there wasn't one. Also, keep a sharp eye on the platform marquees—the information scrolls by quickly and is very easy to miss! 🚉",
